course: geography
lesson: 33
title: Castile, Portugal and the first sea empires
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Portugal and Castile sat on the edge of Europe, facing the open Atlantic, so while Venice owned the old Mediterranean routes, they looked for new ones across the ocean.
explain: Step by step Portuguese ships crept down the coast of Africa, like a child exploring a dark corridor one door at a time, until in 1498 Vasco da Gama reached India by sea.
explain: Columbus sailed west for Castile in 1492 hoping to reach Asia; people already knew the Earth was round, but he thought it was much smaller than it is, and he bumped into the Caribbean instead.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 this is a fun race of explorers, colonists and gold, but for the Taíno, the Aztecs and the Inca it meant conquest, forced labour and deadly new diseases.
explain: The same year, 1492, Granada fell and Jews in Castile and Aragon were ordered to convert or leave; many found new homes in the Ottoman Empire, North Africa and later the Netherlands.
fact: Christian kingdoms in Iberia, the peninsula of Spain and Portugal, including Castile, Aragon and Portugal, slowly took the peninsula from Muslim rulers over centuries, ending with the fall of Granada in 1492.
fact: In 1492 Castile and Aragon ordered Jews to convert or leave, and later Muslims were forced to convert and finally expelled in 1609 to 1614.
fact: Portugal's Prince Henry, called "the Navigator", sponsored voyages down the west coast of Africa from the 1410s, though he did not sail on the long voyages himself.
fact: The Portuguese and Spanish used caravels, small fast ships with triangular lateen sails that could sail closer to the wind.
fact: Bartolomeu Dias sailed round the southern tip of Africa in 1488, and Vasco da Gama reached India by sea in 1498.
fact: Columbus, an Italian sailing for Castile, reached the Caribbean in 1492 while trying to reach Asia by sailing west.
fact: Educated people in Columbus's time already knew the Earth was round, so the story that he proved it is a myth.
fact: The Taíno, the people Columbus met in the Caribbean, were nearly wiped out within a few decades by forced labour, violence and new diseases, though many Caribbean people today have Taíno ancestors.
fact: Words such as hammock, canoe and hurricane came into English from the Taíno language.
fact: Hernán Cortés and a small Spanish force, with tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, conquered the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan in 1521, helped by a smallpox outbreak, and Mexico City was built on its ruins.
fact: Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa in 1532 and had him killed in 1533, even after a huge ransom of gold and silver was paid.
fact: Portugal colonised Brazil from the 1530s, at first for brazilwood, a red dye wood that gave the land its name, and then for sugar plantations worked by enslaved Indigenous people and Africans.
fact: Spain took the Philippines from 1565, and Manila galleons crossed the Pacific between Acapulco and Manila until 1815, carrying American silver one way and Chinese silk and porcelain the other.
exercise: When did Granada, the last Muslim kingdom in Iberia, fall? | 1492 | In 1337 | In 1066 | In 1609
exercise: What were Jews in Castile and Aragon ordered to do in 1492? | Convert or leave | Move to Granada | Join the navy | Pay a small fee and stay
exercise: When were Muslims finally expelled from Spain? | Between 1609 and 1614 | In 1337, before Granada fell | In 1453 | In 1815
exercise: What did Prince Henry "the Navigator" do? | Sponsored voyages down the African coast | Sailed round Africa himself on a caravel | Discovered America before Columbus did | Built the Arsenal of Venice
exercise: Why could caravels sail closer to the wind? | They had triangular lateen sails | They had steam engines below deck | They were pulled by whales | They had no sails at all
exercise: Who first sailed round the southern tip of Africa, in 1488? | Bartolomeu Dias | Vasco da Gama | Christopher Columbus | Ferdinand Magellan
exercise: Who first reached India by sailing around Africa? | Vasco da Gama | Bartolomeu Dias | Christopher Columbus | Francisco Pizarro
exercise: Where was Columbus trying to go in 1492? | To Asia, by sailing west | To the Caribbean, which he knew about | To the South Pole | Round Africa to India
exercise: Did Columbus prove that the Earth was round? | No, educated people already knew it | Yes, everyone thought it was flat until then | Yes, he sailed all the way round it | No, he proved it was flat
exercise: What happened to the Taíno after 1492? | They were nearly wiped out by forced labour, violence and disease | They sailed to Spain and conquered it with the help of the Moors | They moved peacefully to Europe | Nothing changed for them
exercise: Which English word comes from the Taíno language? | Hammock | Windmill | Breakfast | Cathedral
exercise: Why could a small Spanish force defeat the Aztecs? | Tens of thousands of Indigenous allies and disease helped them | The Aztecs had no army at all and had never fought a war before | The Spanish had aeroplanes | The Aztecs invited them to rule
exercise: Which city did Cortés conquer in 1521? | Tenochtitlan | Cusco | Manila | Constantinople
exercise: What did Pizarro do to Atahualpa after the ransom was paid? | He had him killed | He set him free with honour | He made him king of Spain | He took him to Lisbon
exercise: Why is Brazil named after a tree? | Brazilwood was the first thing the Portuguese came for | A Portuguese king was called Brazil | The tree grows only in Portugal and nowhere else | The Taíno named it after their favourite tree
exercise: What were Brazil's sugar plantations worked by? | Enslaved Indigenous people and Africans | Paid workers who sailed out from Portugal | Machines from England | Monks from Venice
exercise: Where did the Manila galleons sail between? | Acapulco and Manila | Lisbon and Brazil | Venice and Crete | London and New York
test: My teacher said Columbus discovered the Earth was round. That right? | No, educated people already knew, he just thought it was smaller | Yes, before 1492 everyone thought it was flat, even the kings | Yes, and Magellan later proved it was shaped like an egg | No, Columbus proved it was flat
test: In EU5 a Portuguese explorer quickly maps the route round Africa. How long did it really take? | Decades, from the 1410s to 1498 | One summer in 1337 | About a week | About three hundred years, from 1200 to 1500
test: A cartoon shows Cortés and a few hundred Spaniards beating the Aztecs alone. What does it leave out? | Tens of thousands of Indigenous allies and diseases | The Aztecs had already left the city and gone north | Cortés had a huge army of cannons and horses only | The Aztecs surrendered at once without any fight at all
test: A Mexican pupil says her city stands where Tenochtitlan stood. Is she right? | Yes, Mexico City was built on its ruins | No, Tenochtitlan was in Peru | No, Tenochtitlan was in Spain | Yes, but only the airport
test: If caravels had only square sails, what would have been harder? | Sailing back against the wind along the African coast | Carrying any cargo at all, even food and water for the crew | Floating on the sea | Fishing near Portugal
test: ugh Pizarro got the gold AND killed the emperor anyway?? | Yes, Atahualpa was killed in 1533 after the ransom was paid | No, he gave all the gold back and sailed home to Spain in shame | No, Atahualpa became a Spanish duke | Yes, but only after 100 years
test: your mate says Brazil speaks Portuguese cos it's near Portugal lol | No, Portugal colonised it from the 1530s | Yes, Brazil is right next to Portugal | No, Brazil was always Spanish | Yes, Portugal is in South America
test: A ship leaves Acapulco loaded with silver and comes back with silk and porcelain. What is it? | A Manila galleon | A Venetian galley | A caravel of Henry the Navigator | A Viking longship
test: Your friend says Henry the Navigator sailed further than anyone. What is wrong? | He paid for the voyages but did not sail on them himself | Nothing, he sailed right round the world twice | He only sailed on rivers | He was not Portuguese
test: 1492 was a big year. Which list is right? | Granada fell, Jews were told to convert or leave, Columbus reached the Caribbean | Constantinople fell to the Ottomans, Venice ended and Granada was founded | Vasco da Gama reached India and Brazil was founded | The Manila galleons stopped sailing
test: If no new diseases had crossed the ocean, what would most likely be different? | Far fewer Indigenous people would have died | Columbus would have reached Asia | Spain would have lost Granada | The Taíno would have spoken Spanish already
exam: Identify the navigator who first attained India by circumnavigating the African continent, together with the year thereof. | Vasco da Gama, in 1498 | Bartolomeu Dias, in 1488 | Christopher Columbus, in 1492 | Hernán Cortés, in 1521
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the knowledge of educated Europeans concerning the shape of the Earth in 1492? | The Earth was already understood to be spherical | The Earth was universally believed to be flat and bounded by an abyss | The Earth was considered to be cubical | The shape of the Earth was wholly unknown
exam: Ascertain the principal factors which enabled a small Spanish force to subjugate Tenochtitlan in 1521. | Numerous Indigenous allies and the ravages of introduced disease | The absence of any Aztec armed resistance | Superior Spanish numbers exceeding those of the Aztecs | The voluntary abdication of the Aztec state to Portugal
exam: State the commodity from which the territory of Brazil derives its name. | Brazilwood | Sugar cane juice | Silver ore | Spices from Asia
